  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study notes that as the volume of labeled training data increases, conventional supervised and tabular models eventually outperform the LLM-based approaches.",
      "what": "This study evaluates three large language model (LLM) frameworks—zero-shot direct prompting, textual-gradient prompt optimization, and supervised prediction using text embeddings—for predicting travel behavior. The frameworks were benchmarked against multinomial logit, random forests, neural networks, and TabPFN using Swissmetro mode choice, London mode choice, and NHTS trip-purpose datasets under varying labeled-data budgets. The results show that LLMs outperform or match traditional tabular baselines in data-scarce environments, and diagnostic tests confirm they respond to underlying travel-time and cost structures rather than relying on memorized benchmarks.",
      "why": "It establishes that LLMs can serve as viable, competitive predictors for travel behavior when labeled data is highly scarce, bypassing the need for extensive manual feature engineering or expert-crafted prompts."
    }
